<p>H.G. Wells&#8217; predictions for the &#8220;house of the future,&#8221; <a href="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v26%201903/page/678/mode/2up">made in 1903</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Chimneys will be obsolete, replaced by an electric heater in the center of each apartment. The absence of chimneys will also permit roof gardens.</li>
<li>Air will be circulated by an electric fan &#8220;and will come in through a proper shaft and filter&#8221; communicating with the roof.</li>
<li>No longer required for ventilation, windows may take any shape and will be positioned for lighting and for aesthetic effect.</li>
<li>Labor-saving appliances will include dustless sweepers, self-making beds, and &#8220;neatly-mounted electric cooking things.&#8221;</li>
<li>Walls and floors will meet in a curve rather than at right angles, to prevent the accumulation of dust.</li>
<li>Walls will be soundproof, doors will be framed in metal, artificial stone floors will be clad in linoleum, and all will be fireproof.</li>
<li>&#8220;In the end our present houses may come to be thought singularly squat, massive, and dark beside these elegant new comers.&#8221;</li>
</ul>
<p>He also thought that single-family homes would become rare in urban areas, replaced with &#8220;a wide forest of buildings in which many families may live,&#8221; sharing common restaurants, children&#8217;s playgrounds, and the like. &#8220;The scope for ingenuity and for invention in planning such places is enormous.&#8221;</p>
